INT. MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - GALLERY OF AUTOMATA - NIGHT

A hushed gallery lined with glass cases. Hundreds of clocks tick out of rhythm: cuckoos, carriage clocks, a grandfather the height of a wardrobe. A mechanical bird in a gilt cage sings once an hour. Moonlight pools on parquet.

At the center, five people in museum staff blazers pretend to inspect a display. They are not staff.

MADAME ODETTE FONTAINE-ABARA, 60s, silver chignon, holds a tiny silver wand like a baton and speaks as if lecturing an audience. THADDEUS "TICK" WILLOUGHBY, 30s, with a tool roll under his blazer, crouches at the grandfather clock. LOTTIE PAXTON, 20s, in sneakers, unspools a coil of fishing line. OLD BERTRAM MULDOON, 70s, with a cane and a hearing aid, taps a case with one knuckle. And JOSEFINA "JOJO" ARRIAGA, 30s, the lookout, stands in the arched doorway pretending to read a pamphlet.

ODETTE
(brightly, to the empty gallery)
And here we have the Marquis automaton, circa 1782, a marvel of pre-Revolutionary engineering.

TICK
(whispering)
She does the voice every time.

LOTTIE
(whispering)
It's the voice that makes it work.

Bertram knocks on the glass of the automaton's case and cups a hand behind his ear. A tiny gear inside shifts. He nods, satisfied. Lottie drops the fishing line through a crack in the display, feeds it down to the latch, and ties a knot in one smooth motion, while Tick unscrews the clock's back panel, reaches in, and removes a small velvet pouch. Jojo, without looking up from her pamphlet, taps twice on the doorframe.

Everyone freezes. Footsteps echo from the far corridor, then fade.

JOJO
Clear.

ODETTE
(back to the lecture voice)
Note the delicate wrist articulation.

The mechanical bird begins to sing. All five heads turn. They stand, perfectly still, until it finishes its song.
